From: Yuichi Kusakabe <yuichi_kusakabe@jp.honda>
Date: Sat, 17 Oct 2026 23:48:05 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] vkr: make the ring thread's busy-wait tunable

Between commands the ring thread polls the ring until idle_timeout has
passed since its last command: vkr_ring_relax() yields 15 times
(2^4 - 1), then sleeps 10 us, and the sleep doubles each time the
count of polls doubles. A guest that submits a frame in several small
batches restarts that pattern for every batch. On the Raspberry Pi 5
the AGL cluster's ring thread (DomU) made 24,000 sched_yield and 9,000
clock_nanosleep calls a second, on DomD's two vCPUs, which a video
decode in DomD also needs.

Read the busy-wait order and the first sleep from VKR_RING_RELAX
("<order 0..4>,<first sleep in us 1..1000>"), once, like
VKR_RING_IDLE_WAIT_MS; a value that does not parse is logged and
ignored. Unset, the values stay 4 and 10. Order 0 means no yields.

The cost is latency. Mesa's guest asks for a 1 ms idle_timeout, so
the ring goes idle about 1.3 ms after its last command and is woken
by the guest's notify from then on, as before. With "0,100" the
sleeps until then are 100, 200, 200, 400 and 400 us, so a command
that arrives in that window waits up to 0.4 ms instead of 40 us.

Measured on the Raspberry Pi 5 (2026-10-17) with "0,100" for both
device models, against upstream's values in the same boot. The
cluster's ring thread made 6,987 to 10,067 sched_yield and about 4,600
clock_nanosleep calls in 3 s against 70,308 to 77,515 and about 27,000
(four 3 s counts each; the yields left are all V3DV's, from the same
thread). H.264 1080p60 playback in a WebView in DomA showed 55.0
frames a second against 52.7 (two alternating sessions, full screen
and HMI each), within the spread of single runs (50 to 57). The
cluster drew 25.1 frames a second against 26.3 (32 counts of 5 s
each). On the Raspberry Pi 4 (2026-10-21) a WebView's 720p60 H.264 in
DomA showed the same frame rates with it and without it; the ring's
calls were not counted there, and that board runs no cluster.

Assisted-by: Claude Code:claude-opus-5-5
Signed-off-by: Yuichi Kusakabe <yuichi_kusakabe@jp.honda>
Upstream-Status: Pending
---
--- a/src/venus/vkr_ring.c
+++ b/src/venus/vkr_ring.c
@@ -270,12 +270,42 @@
    return ns_per_sec * now.tv_sec + now.tv_nsec;
 }
 
+/* 0025: the busy-wait order and the first sleep from VKR_RING_RELAX
+ * ("<order 0..4>,<first sleep in us 1..1000>"), else upstream's 4 and 10.
+ * Read once; the render server inherits the device model's environment. */
+static void
+vkr_ring_relax_params(uint32_t *order, uint32_t *base_us)
+{
+   static atomic_uint_fast32_t cached; /* order + 1 in bits 0-7, base us above */
+   uint32_t v = atomic_load_explicit(&cached, memory_order_relaxed);
+   if (!v) {
+      unsigned long o = 4, us = 10;
+      const char *s = getenv("VKR_RING_RELAX");
+      if (s && *s) {
+         char *end, *end2;
+         const unsigned long a = strtoul(s, &end, 10);
+         const unsigned long b = *end == ',' ? strtoul(end + 1, &end2, 10) : 0;
+         if (end != s && *end == ',' && end2 != end + 1 && !*end2 && a <= 4 && b >= 1 &&
+             b <= 1000) {
+            o = a;
+            us = b;
+         } else {
+            vkr_log("VKR_RING_RELAX=\"%s\" ignored: want \"<order 0..4>,<us 1..1000>\"",
+                    s);
+         }
+      }
+      v = (uint32_t)(o + 1) | (uint32_t)(us << 8);
+      atomic_store_explicit(&cached, v, memory_order_relaxed);
+   }
+   *order = (v & 0xff) - 1;
+   *base_us = v >> 8;
+}
+
 static void
 vkr_ring_relax(uint32_t *iter)
 {
-   /* TODO do better */
-   const uint32_t busy_wait_order = 4;
-   const uint32_t base_sleep_us = 10;
+   uint32_t busy_wait_order, base_sleep_us;
+   vkr_ring_relax_params(&busy_wait_order, &base_sleep_us);
 
    (*iter)++;
    if (*iter < (1u << busy_wait_order)) {
